  "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{1+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 1}}{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 3}}{3} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{(-1)^{4+1}{x}^{2\\cdot 4}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n}}{n}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+{x}^{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
